{# The table's bottom edge and its control: drawn whenever there are rows —
   without it the last row simply falls off the card — and under the table's
   scroll box rather than inside it, so it stays on screen whatever the rows
   are doing. It counts nothing: `20 events` under a page of twenty is a fact
   about a window.

   Newer and Older, not Previous and Next: the list is newest first and the
   cursor walks it in time — `backward` reaches what arrived after these rows
   — and `Previous` on such a list points at whichever end the reader guessed.
   `rel` still says prev and next, because that is what they are to a browser.

   Both directions are always drawn, joined into one control: a footer that
   renders only the link it has moves the other across the row the moment the
   first appears. The direction there is no page in is the same object,
   disabled - and `aria-disabled` rather than `aria-hidden`, so a screen
   reader says the control is there and unavailable rather than not saying it
   is there at all. A pager missing half of itself reads as a pager that only
   goes one way. #}
<nav class="flex shrink-0 items-center justify-center border-t border-base-300 px-4 py-1" aria-label="Pagination" data-testid="do-pager">
  <div class="join" data-testid="do-pager-links">
    {% if params.previousHref %}
      <a class="btn btn-xs join-item" rel="prev" href="{{ params.previousHref }}" data-testid="do-pager-newer">← Newer</a>
    {% else %}
      <span class="btn btn-xs join-item btn-disabled" aria-disabled="true" data-testid="do-pager-newer-disabled">← Newer</span>
    {% endif %}
    {% if params.nextHref %}
      <a class="btn btn-xs join-item" rel="next" href="{{ params.nextHref }}" data-testid="do-pager-older">Older →</a>
    {% else %}
      <span class="btn btn-xs join-item btn-disabled" aria-disabled="true" data-testid="do-pager-older-disabled">Older →</span>
    {% endif %}
  </div>
</nav>
